{
  "documents": [
    {
      "id": "topic_0",
      "text": "Kinematics 2D Vector Decomposition: In 2D projectile motion, any position vector r(t) is decomposed into independent horizontal and vertical axes: r(t) = x(t)i + y(t)j. The horizontal and vertical motions are completely independent of each other.",
      "metadata": {
        "topic": "Kinematics 2D Vector Decomposition",
        "frame_to_display": "frame_000.jpg",
        "math_latex": "\\vec{r}(t) = x(t)\\hat{i} + y(t)\\hat{j}",
        "params": {
          "velocity": 20,
          "angle": 45,
          "gravity": 9.8
        },
        "full_text": "In 2D projectile motion, any position vector r(t) is decomposed into independent horizontal and vertical axes: r(t) = x(t)i + y(t)j. The horizontal and vertical motions are completely independent of each other."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "topic_1",
      "text": "Horizontal Constant Velocity Motion: Because there is zero net force acting horizontally in a vacuum, horizontal acceleration a_x = 0. Therefore, the horizontal velocity component vx remains strictly constant throughout the motion: vx = u*cos(theta), and horizontal displacement is x(t) = (u*cos(theta))*t.",
      "metadata": {
        "topic": "Horizontal Constant Velocity Motion",
        "frame_to_display": "frame_005.jpg",
        "math_latex": "a_x = 0 \\implies v_x = u\\cos\\theta, \\quad x(t) = (u\\cos\\theta)t",
        "params": {
          "velocity": 25,
          "angle": 30,
          "gravity": 9.8
        },
        "full_text": "Because there is zero net force acting horizontally in a vacuum, horizontal acceleration a_x = 0. Therefore, the horizontal velocity component vx remains strictly constant throughout the motion: vx = u*cos(theta), and horizontal displacement is x(t) = (u*cos(theta))*t."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "topic_2",
      "text": "Vertical Motion Under Gravitational Acceleration: Gravity acts exclusively along the downward vertical axis with acceleration a_y = -g (-9.8 m/s^2). The vertical velocity continuously decreases as the object ascends: vy(t) = u*sin(theta) - g*t. At the apex (maximum height), vertical velocity vy is instantaneous zero.",
      "metadata": {
        "topic": "Vertical Motion Under Gravitational Acceleration",
        "frame_to_display": "frame_010.jpg",
        "math_latex": "a_y = -g \\implies v_y(t) = u\\sin\\theta - gt, \\quad y(t) = (u\\sin\\theta)t - \\frac{1}{2}gt^2",
        "params": {
          "velocity": 30,
          "angle": 60,
          "gravity": 9.8
        },
        "full_text": "Gravity acts exclusively along the downward vertical axis with acceleration a_y = -g (-9.8 m/s^2). The vertical velocity continuously decreases as the object ascends: vy(t) = u*sin(theta) - g*t. At the apex (maximum height), vertical velocity vy is instantaneous zero."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "topic_3",
      "text": "Total Time of Flight and Apex Height: The total Time of Flight T is obtained by solving for when vertical displacement y(t) returns to 0: T = (2*u*sin(theta))/g. The maximum peak height H_max reached by the projectile is H_max = (u^2 * sin^2(theta))/(2*g).",
      "metadata": {
        "topic": "Total Time of Flight and Apex Height",
        "frame_to_display": "frame_020.jpg",
        "math_latex": "T = \\frac{2u\\sin\\theta}{g}, \\quad H_{max} = \\frac{u^2\\sin^2\\theta}{2g}",
        "params": {
          "velocity": 28,
          "angle": 45,
          "gravity": 9.8
        },
        "full_text": "The total Time of Flight T is obtained by solving for when vertical displacement y(t) returns to 0: T = (2*u*sin(theta))/g. The maximum peak height H_max reached by the projectile is H_max = (u^2 * sin^2(theta))/(2*g)."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "topic_4",
      "text": "Horizontal Range and Maximum Angle: The total horizontal distance covered before landing is the Range R = (u^2 * sin(2*theta))/g. In a vacuum, maximum horizontal range R_max is achieved at an angle of 45 degrees. Complementary launch angles (e.g., 30 deg and 60 deg) yield the identical horizontal range.",
      "metadata": {
        "topic": "Horizontal Range and Maximum Angle",
        "frame_to_display": "frame_030.jpg",
        "math_latex": "R = \\frac{u^2\\sin(2\\theta)}{g} \\implies R_{max} \\text{ at } \\theta = 45^\\circ",
        "params": {
          "velocity": 25,
          "angle": 45,
          "gravity": 9.8
        },
        "full_text": "The total horizontal distance covered before landing is the Range R = (u^2 * sin(2*theta))/g. In a vacuum, maximum horizontal range R_max is achieved at an angle of 45 degrees. Complementary launch angles (e.g., 30 deg and 60 deg) yield the identical horizontal range."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "topic_5",
      "text": "Air Resistance and Drag Tangent: When air resistance (aerodynamic drag) is introduced, the projectile trajectory is no longer a symmetrical parabola. The horizontal velocity slows down faster, reducing the maximum range, and the optimal launch angle drops slightly below 45 degrees.",
      "metadata": {
        "topic": "Air Resistance and Drag Tangent",
        "frame_to_display": "frame_015.jpg",
        "math_latex": "\\vec{F}_{drag} = -\\frac{1}{2}\\rho C_d A v^2 \\hat{v}",
        "params": {
          "velocity": 25,
          "angle": 38,
          "gravity": 9.8
        },
        "full_text": "When air resistance (aerodynamic drag) is introduced, the projectile trajectory is no longer a symmetrical parabola. The horizontal velocity slows down faster, reducing the maximum range, and the optimal launch angle drops slightly below 45 degrees."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "topic_6",
      "text": "Velocity Vector Decomposition and Kinetic Energy: The magnitude of the instantaneous speed at any time is given by |v| = sqrt(vx^2 + vy^2). The angle of the velocity vector with respect to the horizontal is tan(alpha) = vy / vx. Kinetic energy is minimum at the peak where vertical velocity is zero.",
      "metadata": {
        "topic": "Velocity Vector Decomposition and Kinetic Energy",
        "frame_to_display": "frame_025.jpg",
        "math_latex": "|\\vec{v}(t)| = \\sqrt{v_x^2 + v_y(t)^2}, \\quad \\tan\\alpha = \\frac{v_y}{v_x}",
        "params": {
          "velocity": 22,
          "angle": 50,
          "gravity": 9.8
        },
        "full_text": "The magnitude of the instantaneous speed at any time is given by |v| = sqrt(vx^2 + vy^2). The angle of the velocity vector with respect to the horizontal is tan(alpha) = vy / vx. Kinetic energy is minimum at the peak where vertical velocity is zero."
      }
    }
  ],
